cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "contract-bus: the weft harness, a command loop over iceoryx2"
abstract: >
  The contract a transport layer and an interactor compose against: a command in,
  reply bytes out, over iceoryx2 shared memory with no daemon and no copy. Two
  pub/sub services carry the pair, correlated by an eight-byte request id, because
  the generated ABI surface lists only the pub/sub builders and adding
  request-response would mean extending that table unverified. Every plane and
  every edge links this library and none of them links iceoryx2: the C ABI is
  named in a signature file and turned into a dlsym dispatch table, so a missing
  bus is a start-up failure with a message rather than a link error, and the build
  graph holds no Rust artifact.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/contract-bus"
keywords:
  - shared-memory
  - zero-copy
  - inter-process-communication
  - iceoryx2

references:

  # ── The bus itself, dlopened rather than linked ──────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "Eclipse iceoryx2: true zero-copy inter-process communication"
    authors:
      - name: "Eclipse iceoryx contributors"
    year: 2026
    version: v0.9.3
    license: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
    url: "https://github.com/eclipse-iceoryx/iceoryx2"
    notes: >
      Not vendored and not linked. `iceoryx2.sigs` names the C ABI functions the
      harness calls and the generated table dlopens the library at start, so this
      repository's build graph holds no Rust artifact at all. v0.9.3 is the version
      both proof runs were made against: Windows 11 needed two workarounds, one of
      them iceoryx2's own hardcoded `C:\Temp` (issue 1868, fixed after v0.9.3);
      macOS 26.5.2 arm64 needed none and built clean from source.

  # ── Vendored trees, each stating its own terms ───────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "generate_stubs.py, from the Chromium source tree"
    authors:
      - name: "The Chromium Authors"
    year: 2012
    license: BSD-3-Clause
    url: "https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/tools/generate_stubs/"
    notes: >
      Vendored at `thirdparty/generate_stubs`, with Chromium's LICENSE beside it.
      Turns `iceoryx2.sigs` into the dlsym dispatch table. It emits no prototypes,
      because Chromium's own callers include the real library headers and this
      repository keeps none on the path, so `sigs_to_header.py` emits those from
      the same signature file.
  - type: software
    title: "dlfcn-win32"
    authors:
      - family-names: Polla
        given-names: Ramiro
      - family-names: Gu
        given-names: "Tiancheng (Timothy)"
      - family-names: "Rohár"
        given-names: Pali
      - family-names: Habacker
        given-names: Ralf
    year: 2020
    version: v1.4.2
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/dlfcn-win32/dlfcn-win32"
    notes: >
      Vendored at `thirdparty/dlfcn-win32`, compiled only behind `if(WIN32)`.
      MinGW ships no `<dlfcn.h>` and the generated stub table needs one; this
      implements dlopen/dlsym/dlclose over LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress, which left
      the generated code unchanged. Linux and macOS use the platform's own header.
